@node dokgen_diagrams
@section (dokgen diagrams)

@c %start of fragment

This module turns the mermaid diagrams a documentation string carries
into images the manual can show.

A diagram is written in a docstring as a fenced block, the way a
markdown reader already knows.  What follows is one, and it is drawn
where the manual shows this module:

@image{diagrams/dokgen_diagrams-bc388dc4,,,Mermaid diagram}

The fence is consumed, the diagram is written next to the generated
Texinfo as @file{diagrams/NAME-HASH.mmd}, and a renderer turns it into
@file{diagrams/NAME-HASH.png}, which the API chapter references with
@code{@@image}.  The name carries a digest of the diagram's own text, so
a diagram that changed gets a file of its own and an old rendering can
never be shown as the current one.

The renderer is a command, not a library: mermaid is JavaScript, and a
documentation generator that embedded a browser to draw an arrow would
be a worse trade than one that calls the tool a project already uses.
The command @code{mmdc} (from @code{mermaid-cli}) is the default; the
parameters @code{*mermaid-command*} and @code{*diagram-destination*} say
what to run and where to write, so a caller sets them once around a
whole run.  A command left empty renders nothing, which is how a tree
that commits its images builds its manual without a renderer.

When there is no image, the diagram's source is written into the manual
instead, in an @code{@@example} block with a line saying what it is.  A
diagram that silently disappeared from a manual would be worse than one
a reader has to copy.


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@subsection Parameters

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_*diagram-destination*}
@defvr Parameter *diagram-destination*
Default value:

@verbatim 
"manual/api"
@end verbatim

@end defvr


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_*mermaid-command*}
@defvr Parameter *mermaid-command*
Default value:

@verbatim 
""
@end verbatim

@end defvr


@c %end of fragment

@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@subsection Procedures

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_diagram-blocks}
@deffn Procedure diagram-blocks docstring
Return the documentation string split into prose and diagrams.

@strong{docstring} (string): The documentation of a module or of one of
its objects.

@strong{Return value} (list of pairs): Every pair is either @code{(text
. STRING)} or @code{(diagram .  STRING)}, in the order they are written.
A fence that is never closed stays prose, because a docstring with a
typo in it should still reach the reader, and so does one written inside
a Texinfo block that quotes it.

@end deffn


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_diagram-stem}
@deffn Procedure diagram-stem owner source
Return the name a diagram is written under, without an extension.

@strong{owner} (string): What holds the diagram: a procedure's name, or
a module's name.

@strong{source} (string): The diagram, as mermaid.

@strong{Return value} (string): The owner, with every character a shell
or a URL would have to escape replaced by a dash, and a digest of the
diagram.

@end deffn


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_diagram-stexi}
@deffn Procedure diagram-stexi source owner
Return the stexi nodes that show the diagram @var{source}.

@strong{source} (string): The diagram, as mermaid.

@strong{owner} (string): What holds the diagram; see
@code{diagram-stem}.

@strong{Return value} (list of stexi): One node holding the image, in a
paragraph of its own because Texinfo writes an image inline where it
stands, or two nodes holding the diagram's source and a line that says
what it is when no image could be made.

@end deffn


@c %end of fragment

@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

@subsection Variables

@c %start of fragment

@anchor{dokgen_diagrams_DEFAULT_MERMAID_COMMAND}
@defvr Variable DEFAULT_MERMAID_COMMAND
@verbatim 
"mmdc --input %i --output %o.png"
@end verbatim

@end defvr


@c %end of fragment

@c %end of fragment

